part.text:
Fixed the Set-Cookie merge bug and added regression coverage.

## Changes

**`src/context.ts:414`** — `Context.res` setter
- Set-Cookie headers from the existing response are now collected once, outside the header loop.
- The loop skips `set-cookie` rather than deleting the incoming response's cookies.
- Both sets are merged: existing cookies first, then the new response's cookies.
- Content-Type handling and all other header precedence remain unchanged.

```ts
const cookies = this.#res.headers.getSetCookie()
for (const [k, v] of this.#res.headers.entries()) {
  if (k === 'content-type' || k === 'set-cookie') {
    continue
  }
  _res.headers.set(k, v)
}
if (cookies.length > 0) {
  const newCookies = _res.headers.getSetCookie()
  _res.headers.delete('set-cookie')
  for (const cookie of [...cookies, ...newCookies]) {
    _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
  }
}
```

**`src/context.test.ts:430`**
- Updated the existing re-assignment test to expect the new response's cookie along with the existing ones.
- Added a regression test at `src/context.test.ts:448` that simulates middleware setting a cookie on `c.res` and a handler returning a new `Response` with its own `Set-Cookie`.

